From 13eaad49ffca32ee48ed6ec0303415f568bf55bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karsten Sperling <ksperling@apple.com>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 23:12:12 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] EAP-TLS server: Add support for client cert pinning

Allow the hostapd EAP-TLS server to authenticate clients against a
certificate allow-list in addition to, or instead of, PKI trust-chain
validation. Pinning is configured via the per-BSS eap_tls_cert_pinning
option:
  0 = disabled (default, normal PKI validation only)
  1 = require an allow-list match in addition to trust-chain validation
  2 = pin only; skip trust-chain validation and accept any leaf whose
      SHA-256 is in the allow-list (no ca_cert required)

The allow-list reuses the existing EAP user database: each authorized
client is a "cert-sha256-<hex>" entry based on the SHA-256 hash of the
client (leaf) certificate in DER form. An exact (non-wildcard) match is
required so that a "*" entry won't authorize arbitrary certificates.
Pinning behaves identically whether EAP is terminated by the integrated
authenticator or the RADIUS server.

When pinning is enabled, it supersedes check_cert_subject and the
cert-<serial> lookup: When the full leaf certificate is pinned via its
hash, additionally validating the subject field or performing another
lookup via the serial number is superfluous. The serial lookup would
also clobber the sm->user record that was already populated by the
pinning code.

Pin-only mode utilizes a new TLS_CONN_DISABLE_CHAIN_VALIDATION flag. The
peer certificate is retrieved via a new tls_connection_get_peer_cert()
function.  Both are implemented for the OpenSSL and wolfSSL backends.

The feature is guarded by the CONFIG_EAP_TLS_PINNING build flag; builds
against other TLS backends (GnuTLS, internal) are not supported and will
fail to link with the feature enabled.

The added hwsim tests pass with both OpenSSL and wolfSSL.

Signed-off-by: Karsten Sperling <ksperling@apple.com>
